or All Chows (4 chows + pair) which is 2 points. But I also have J2, which is honor, so if I keep it, I might get Dragon Pung if I get more J2, but only one J2 left (unshown J2: 3, but one is in my hand, so 2 left). Or I could discard J2 to simplify to all simples (no honors) for 1 point, but then I need to ensure no terminals either (B1 is terminal, B9 is terminal). So All Simples would require discarding B1,B9,J2.  \n\nBetter to think about quick hand: I have one meld already (chow). I need three more melds and a pair. Possible melds:  \n- Chow from T2,T3,T4 (need to complete as a set, but it's already three tiles, so that's a concealed chow if I declare it when winning).  \n- Chow from B1,B2 waiting for B3.  \n- Chow from B5,B6 waiting for B4 or B7.  \n- Pair from B9 (already a pair).  \n\nSo I already have: concealed chow (T2,T3,T4), another potential chow (B1,B2,B3), another (B5,B6,B7), and pair B9. That would be four chows + pair = All Chows (2 points). Plus maybe other patterns: Half Flush? No, because I have B suit (Dots) and T suit (Bamboo) and honor J2, so not one suit. Mixed Straight? Possibly if I get chows in different suits: but my chows are in T and B, not all three suits.  \n\nBut to complete the hand, I need B3 or B4/B7. Unshown B3: 4, B4:4, B7:2. So B7 is relatively scarce (only 2 left). B3 and B4 are plenty.  \n\nAlso I have T5 which is isolated; could be discarded. Or T4 is also isolated? Actually T4 is part of T2,T3,T4 chow, so keep T4.  \n\nSo promising discards: T5 (since it's isolated Bamboo 5, and I already have a meld using T5, so extra T5 not helpful), or J2 (honor, hard to get pair/pung), or B1 (terminal, but useful for chow with B2).  \n\nConsidering safety: Look at opponents' discards and melds.  \n- Next player meld: B8 pung, so they likely don't need B8, but maybe need other Bamboo? Their history: played F1, J3, F2, B7, Peng B8, F3, T1, W4. They discarded T1 and W4, so maybe they don't need Bamboo or Characters? Hard to say.  \n- Across player meld: B5,B6,B7 chow, so they have Dots sequence. Their history: Play J1, F2, Chi B6, W9, B2, W5, F1. They discarded B2 and W5, so maybe they don't need low Dots or mid Characters.  \n- Previous player meld: J3 pung, so they have honors. Their history: Play J1, F1, Peng J3, T5, T9, B8, B7, F4, F2. They discarded T5 and T9, so maybe they don't need Bamboo.  \n\nSo discarding Bamboo tiles might be relatively safe, especially T5 since T5 was discarded by previous player. Also T9 discarded by previous player. So T5 seems safe.  \n\nDiscarding J2: J2 is Green Dragon; no one has melded it, but J1 and J3 have been played. J2 might be safe? But unshown J2: 3, so there are two left, someone might be collecting dragons. Previous player has J3 pung, so maybe they want dragons? But they discarded J1 and F1, so maybe not.  \n\nDiscarding B1: B1 is terminal Dot; across player discarded B2, so maybe they don't need B1. But B1 is terminal, could be dangerous for someone waiting for terminals. Unshown B1: 4, so plenty.  \n\nDiscarding B9: I have a pair of B9, so discarding one would break the pair, leaving me without a natural pair. But B9 is terminal, might be dangerous. Unshown B9: 4, so many left.  \n\nI think keeping the B9 pair is good because it can be my pair. So I shouldn't discard B9.  \n\nThus, best discards are between T5, J2, B1, B2, T2, T3, T4, B5, B6. Among these, T5 is redundant and safe. J2 is honor and not contributing to sequences. B1 is terminal but part of a potential chow.  \n\nI want to maximize hand potential. If I discard J2, I move towards All Chows and possibly All Simples (if I also discard B1 and B9 later). But All Simples requires no terminals and no honors, so I'd need to discard B1 and B9 as well, which might be tough. Alternatively, I could aim for Mixed Straight (chows 123, 456, 789 in different suits) but I have T2,T3,T4 (Bamboo 234) and B5,B6 (Dots 56) and B1,B2 (Dots 12) \u2013 not matching.  \n\nActually, for Mixed Straight, I need chows: 123 in one suit, 456 in another, 789 in third. I have T2,T3,T4 (234) not 123. So not ideal.  \n\nMaybe aim for Pure Straight (123,456,789 in same suit) but I have two suits.  \n\nGiven I already have a meld in Bamboo, and many Bamboo tiles, maybe I should focus on Bamboo suit to try for Half Flush (one suit + honors). But I have many Dots, so would require discarding all Dots, which is many tiles. Not feasible quickly.  \n\nSo probably best to go for a simple hand like All Chows. For that, I need to complete two more chows: one from B1,B2 (need B3) and one from B5,B6 (need B4 or B7). And keep the pair B9. So I should keep B1,B2,B5,B6,B9 pair, and T2,T3,T4 (which is already a chow). So discard the odd tiles: T5 and J2. Between them, T5 is safer based on discards.  \n\nAlso, T5 is close to my meld T4,T5,T6, so discarding T5 might be less likely to give someone a chow? Actually, someone might need T5 for a chow with T3,T4 or T4,T6 etc. But previous player discarded T5, so it might be safe.  \n\nI'll discard T5.  \n\nReason: T5 is isolated in my hand, safe based on previous player's discard, and discarding it keeps my potential chows intact for an All Chows hand.  \n\nAnswer: Play T5."}
